  "semantic_checklist": [
    {
      "id": "C01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The attached printed page 4 displays (1.10) in Section 1.1.1 and explains conserved density and endpoint flux. Printed pages 1, 3, and 5 supply vector-state, flux, and discontinuity context.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation concerns spatially integrated vector density, endpoint flux, rectangle conservation, and its time derivative.",
      "reasoning": "The selected passage and inherited context match the objects being compared. Source and translation hashes are supplied identifiers; they were not independently recomputed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 1 introduces q: ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m; pages 3–4 use a state-dependent vector flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "The binders are natural m, q: ℝ × ℝ → ℝ^m, and flux: ℝ^m → ℝ^m, with real endpoints.",
      "reasoning": "The real finite-dimensional state and state-dependent flux types agree. Allowing zero coordinates adds a harmless empty-vector case."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) applies to any two spatial points. The recorded user interpretation explicitly places almost-everywhere time validity after fixing the spatial interval.",
      "translation_evidence": "A–C quantify all applicable endpoints; D quantifies a and b before almost every t.",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted interpretation, endpoint scope and interval-dependent exceptional sets are preserved."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The printed balance describes homogeneous conservation. The recorded interpretation adopts rectangle conservation with explicit integrability conditions.",
      "translation_evidence": "A requires spatial interval integrability at every time; B requires temporal interval integrability of each endpoint flux; C is rectangle balance. No state smoothness is assumed.",
      "reasoning": "These are appropriate explicit conditions for the adopted rectangle formulation. They are additional interpretive conditions, not hypotheses enumerated in the printed display."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) gives the time derivative of spatial mass as endpoint flux difference; the recorded interpretation adopts rectangle conservation and intervalwise almost-everywhere mass rate.",
      "translation_evidence": "The proposition is (A ∧ B ∧ C) ↔ (A ∧ B ∧ C ∧ D), where D is the mass-rate identity using μ.",
      "reasoning": "The required balance and derivative conclusions are present under the adopted interpretation, but identifying the integrals and exceptional sets with their intended measure remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source writes ordinary spatial integration dx and temporal differentiation d/dt. The recorded convention adds temporal integration and almost-everywhere validity.",
      "translation_evidence": "D015 interprets an ordinary real derivative; D083 specifies oriented integrals; D065 explicitly leaves the real volume measure and its normalization unidentified.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied ledger explains the derivative, orientation, conjunctions, vector operations, and structural dependencies. It does not establish that μ is standard Lebesgue measure. This is a substantive unresolved operator dependency."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) is an equality, with flux at x₁ minus flux at x₂. The adopted interpretation retains rectangle conservation alongside the derivative identity.",
      "translation_evidence": "C and D use exact equalities with the same sign; both sides of the equivalence retain A, B, and C.",
      "reasoning": "The connective introduces no claim that an almost-everywhere derivative identity alone recovers rectangle conservation. Its substantive direction adds D to the balance property."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "q is conserved density, its spatial integral is total amount, and f(q) is endpoint flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "M_ab(u) integrates q over space; its derivative is flux(q(a,t)) − flux(q(b,t)).",
      "reasoning": "Density, integrated amount, time variable, and composed endpoint flux retain their distinct roles."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) has unit coefficients and the endpoint order x₁ then x₂.",
      "translation_evidence": "The displayed coefficients and endpoint order match, but D065 does not identify the normalization of μ.",
      "reasoning": "There is no explicit coefficient or indexing discrepancy. An unknown measure normalization can change the numerical content of the integral identities, so this check cannot fully pass."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The inherited state domain is ℝ × ℝ with finitely many real components, and the flux may be nonlinear.",
      "translation_evidence": "The target covers arbitrary finite real dimension, all real space and time arguments, and arbitrary state-to-flux maps subject to the balance property.",
      "reasoning": "No scalar, linear-flux, ordered-endpoint, or smooth-solution specialization is introduced. The zero-dimensional extension does not remove any source case."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source allows any two endpoints and asserts continued integral conservation at discontinuities without defining exceptional sets. The adopted interpretation permits an interval-dependent temporal null set.",
      "translation_evidence": "Oriented integrals handle reversed and coincident endpoints; D allows a μ-null exceptional set for each a,b.",
      "reasoning": "The endpoint cases and placement of exceptional sets fit the adopted interpretation. Whether μ-null means the intended Lebesgue-null remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source discusses conservation of possibly nonzero densities and includes constant-velocity advection.",
      "translation_evidence": "The balance conditions constrain arbitrary q and flux; all relevant integrals carry integrability conditions. Positive dimensions are included.",
      "reasoning": "The proposition is not confined to the empty-vector case or false premises. Zero state and zero flux satisfy its conditions; with the intended Lebesgue measure, constant nonzero states also provide nontrivial conserved mass examples. The reverse implication being immediate does not make the forward derivative assertion vacuous."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected result is the continuous integral conservation law (1.10), preceding the finite volume method discussion.",
      "translation_evidence": "No discretization, recurrence, or execution process is asserted.",
      "reasoning": "There is no selected numerical algorithm whose construction must be preserved."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The paragraph after (1.10) explains conservation separately for each component of q.",
      "translation_evidence": "Finite vectors use coordinatewise arithmetic and vector equality, with the finite product topology for differentiation.",
      "reasoning": "Vector equality and differentiation preserve componentwise conservation. The unexpanded numerical norm formula does not affect this claim: no norm-dependent bound is asserted, and the finite product topology is specified."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) states exact conservation, without error or stability bounds.",
      "translation_evidence": "A–D contain integrability, exact balance, and differentiation only.",
      "reasoning": "No error or stability notion is being compared."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The quantities in (1.10) are the state, its exact spatial integral, and physical endpoint flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation uses q, exact vector integrals, and flux(q); it introduces no approximate or computed quantities.",
      "reasoning": "Exact quantity roles are preserved."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source displays a time derivative, without an asymptotic approximation.",
      "translation_evidence": "The little-o expression appears only as the mathematical definition of the ordinary derivative in D.",
      "reasoning": "The limit expresses exact differentiability, not truncation or a first-order approximation substituted for conservation."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source requires sufficient smoothness for passage to the classical PDE, while the adopted interpretation uses rectangle conservation and almost-everywhere mass rate at discontinuities.",
      "translation_evidence": "The target assumes slice integrability and rectangle balance, derives a derivative of integrated mass, and asserts no differentiability of q, existence, or uniqueness.",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded interpretation, no classical regularity restriction is incorrectly imposed on the integral law. The receipt's separate requirement concerning classical solutionhood is outside this selected equation's target."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted rectangle and intervalwise almost-everywhere interpretation, A–C provide the adopted conservation property and D supplies the intended mass-rate identity with the correct endpoints, signs, and quantifier order. However, the translation explicitly leaves μ unidentified. Without knowing that its integration and null sets use standard Lebesgue measure, these assertions cannot be identified with the interpreted source balance. This is an evidence gap, not an established counterexample to the actual target."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded user-adopted interpretation and standard Lebesgue measure, rectangle conservation with the specified slice integrability yields the almost-everywhere derivative of mass for each fixed spatial interval; retaining A–C on the right makes the reverse half of the translated equivalence immediate. The available translation does not establish that its μ is that measure, so the interpreted source cannot yet be shown to imply the exact translated proposition. The printed source alone also does not specify the adopted analytic conventions."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-measure-semantics",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) uses ordinary spatial integration; the recorded interpretation adopts rectangle integration and intervalwise almost-everywhere mass rate.",
      "translation_evidence": "D065 and the translation's first ambiguity state that Real.measureSpace is exposed only through an inferred instance, leaving μ and its normalization unverified.",
      "impact": "The measure controls spatial mass, temporal flux integrals, and exceptional sets. Both implication directions require adjudication of this dependency before acceptance."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-qualified-comparison",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 4–5 leave the derivative sense and discontinuity conventions unspecified. The separately supplied user receipt explicitly adopts rectangle conservation with integrability and interval-dependent almost-everywhere mass rate.",
      "translation_evidence": "A–C encode the rectangle property and D encodes the intervalwise almost-everywhere derivative; no classical solution predicate appears.",
      "impact": "Apart from unresolved measure semantics, the formulation matches the recorded interpretation. Any eventual acceptance must remain qualified by that interpretation and must not attribute its conventions to the printed source."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Inspection of the attached primary-source pages supports the source contract's extraction of (1.10), its vector and endpoint-flux meaning, and its unresolved treatment of discontinuities. Under the explicitly adopted interpretation, the translation preserves the substantive balance, integrability conditions, derivative placement, and interval-dependent exceptional sets. Its equivalence correctly retains rectangle conservation rather than recovering it from an almost-everywhere derivative alone. The supplied dependency ledger nevertheless explicitly leaves the underlying real measure unidentified. Resolving that evidence gap is necessary for both implications; therefore the judgment is undetermined and requires adjudication."
}
